  The rest of bdj/jvm/ (interpreter, loader, heap, threads, VFS and the other
  natives, except natives/libbluray.ts and the libbluray-derived intrinsics
  above) is original code. The engine's intrinsics for class-library methods
  are reimplementations of specified behaviour: SHA-1 compression
  (natives/intrinsics/crypto.ts), BigInteger.implMulAdd's word
  multiply-accumulate (crypto.ts), PNGImageDecoder.update_crc
  (the PNG specification's CRC-32, png.ts), the java.lang.String methods,
  StringBuilder.append(String), Integer.parseInt, Character.digit and the UTF-8
  decoder's ASCII copy (strings.ts), Math.min/max, Arrays.copyOfRange and
  new String(char[], int, int) (lang.ts), Arrays.fill (arrays.ts) and
  IndexColorModel.getRGB's palette lookup (color-model.ts). Intrinsics that
  derive from OpenJDK's code (DirectColorModel.getRGB, Rectangle.intersection,
  PNGImageDecoder.produceImage's row loop, the jar and manifest scanning of
  jar-text.ts) are not part of the engine: they
  live with the class library in src/bdj-classlib/intrinsics/, under GPLv2
  with the Classpath Exception, and a JVM gets them only through
  JvmOptions.extraIntrinsics (the BD-J worker's entry passes them).
